<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaysone Phomvihane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaysone Phomvihane was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lao-peoples-revolutionary-party">Lao People's Revolutionary Party</a> and of the communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a> movement. When the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed in December 1975 he became prime minister, holding the post until 1991, and then president until his death. In 1986 he launched market-oriented economic reforms.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kaysone Phomvihane was born on 13 December 1920 in Naxeng village, Savannakhet Province, in what was then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>. His father, Nguyễn Trí Loan, a Vietnamese, was a civil servant in the French colonial administration, and his mother, Nang Dok, was a Lao farmer. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, first medicine and then law, without completing a degree. He later said he became a revolutionary at sixteen out of patriotism. In Hanoi he joined student activities linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> and protested against the Japanese occupation, and he received his first military and political training from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 23 August 1945 Kaysone took part in seizing power from the Japanese in Savannakhet. On 20 January 1949 he founded the Lao Issara army, forerunner of the Lao People's Army, and in August 1950 became Minister of Defence in the resistance government formed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lao-peoples-revolutionary-party">Lao People's Party</a> was founded on 22 March 1955 at Nameo village and elected him general secretary. Although Souphanouvong was the movement's public leader, real power lay with Kaysone. After fighting resumed in 1964 he moved the leadership into caves at Viengxay, where they withstood American bombing. The party was renamed the Lao People's Revolutionary Party in 1972.</p><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> in April 1975 the party took power with little bloodshed. On 2 December 1975 Kaysone presided over the session that proclaimed the Lao People's Democratic Republic and became its first prime minister. He kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> closely allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and sided with it in the split with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1978. At the Fourth Party Congress in November 1986 he launched chintanakan mai, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-economic-mechanism">New Economic Mechanism</a>, which gradually opened the economy to market forces within a socialist framework.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In October 1990 three officials who had called for a multiparty system and free elections were arrested; Kaysone held to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> and the party's monopoly of power. At the Fifth Party Congress in March 1991 he became party chairman, and in August 1991 the Supreme People's Assembly adopted the first constitution and elected him president. He died in Vientiane on 21 November 1992, aged 71, and was given a state funeral with Buddhist rites.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> he is revered as a Father of the Nation. Statues of him were placed in every provincial capital, and a museum in his honour was built in Vientiane. In January 2016 the party's Tenth Congress placed Kaysone Phomvihane Thought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> as its ideological foundation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaysone Phomvihane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Pathet Lao gained control of the coalition government formed under the 1973 accords, King Savang Vatthana abdicated, and on 2 December 1975 the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed in Vientiane, with Prince Souphanouvong as president and <b>Kaysone Phomvihane</b> as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-peoples-democratic-republic">Lao People's Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kaysone Phomvihane, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Vientiane, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.55,104.75&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Kaysone Phomvihane on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7402 1549 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kaysone Phomvihane"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7852 1819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7852 1819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.55,104.75&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
